5
00:00:55,822 --> 00:00:57,020
Aditi is in the middle.

6
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:02,520
Aditi Raghunathan is an assistant professor in the Computer Science Department, and she She's my advisor.

7
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:17,840
And second to the right is Vince Konitzer, professor of computer science, head of Focal, and head of the new CoreSafe AI Research Center here at CMU, and also advisor to Xander Hecate, one of the founders of this organization.

8
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:20,840
And with that, I'd like to introduce myself.

9
00:01:21,006 --> 00:01:25,320
I'm Lawrence, I'm the former president of the Carnegie AI Safety Initiative.

10
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:32,200
We're a student organization dedicated toward getting people to think deeply about how AI is going to transform society.

11
00:01:34,222 --> 00:01:37,880
One of these panels aimed at doing that and getting students involved.

12
00:01:38,126 --> 00:01:44,580
We also help facilitate research and get people into the very important field of AI safety.

13
00:01:46,446 --> 00:01:48,100
All right, so we're going to stop at 6.15.

14
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:50,780
Afterwards, we'll have apple cider donuts at the front.

15
00:01:53,806 --> 00:02:01,760
You should come and talk to us, people running this event, Cassie folk, and if there are Any faculty who'd like to stick around, you're welcome to talk to them.

16
00:02:02,062 --> 00:02:04,520
Of course, you guys are not obligated to stay.

17
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:07,180
Yes.

18
00:02:07,758 --> 00:02:08,068
All right.

19
00:02:10,798 --> 00:02:11,120
Okay.

20
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:23,400
So the first question I want to ask is, roughly along the lines of, what does the world look like to you today?

21
00:02:23,694 --> 00:02:30,120
So I started off at CMU right here in 15-122, where I learned how to program in C.

22
00:02:31,022 --> 00:02:35,364
And in that first semester, year, halfway through, ChatGPT was released.

23
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:43,200
And in fact, one of my 122 TAs showed me in office hours the ability of ChatGPT to kind of do my homework.

24
00:02:45,006 --> 00:02:47,980
But today, I actually, like, don't write any code at all.

25
00:02:48,494 --> 00:02:52,740
My friends who have graduated and gone into SWE or Quant, they don't write any code.

26
00:02:53,440 --> 00:03:01,580
Of course, coding is not the goal of doing CS or machine learning or mathematics here, but certainly we're offloading more and more of what we used to do to AI.

27
00:03:02,414 --> 00:03:07,000
So, just to start, I want to ask, how are you and your students using AI in teaching and research?

28
00:03:07,662 --> 00:03:12,360
And in the last four years, how do you think that work has changed?

29
00:03:13,262 --> 00:03:15,100
And anyone can start.

30
00:03:20,302 --> 00:03:22,250
I think, for me, it's the sun, yeah.

31
00:03:23,406 --> 00:03:25,220
For me, I think it varies in my group.

32
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:27,370
Different students take different approaches to it.

33
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:34,710
They still do a lot of it themselves, but it's easy to tell that some rely more on AI for just structuring, running experiments and so on.

34
00:03:35,010 --> 00:03:36,230
Some do it more themselves.

35
00:03:37,330 --> 00:03:43,390
We had one paper that was all done by hand and I think it was a nice paper but didn't have any very deep results.

36
00:03:44,030 --> 00:03:45,110
And then the reviews came back.

37
00:03:45,198 --> 00:03:47,230
It's like, it's a nice paper but it doesn't have any deep results.

38
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:49,010
Now we have a deep result thanks to AI.

39
00:03:49,530 --> 00:03:51,650
So we think we're in better shape there.

40
00:03:53,070 --> 00:03:54,670
For myself, I actually don't.

41
00:03:55,310 --> 00:04:01,630
I play around with it a lot, but for actual writing, I don't use it at all because even if it writes great, it's not what I want to write.

42
00:04:02,350 --> 00:04:10,810
So I think that's an important thing as well, that, of course, by handing things off you might lose some control of what you're actually producing as well.

43
00:04:11,278 --> 00:04:19,870
So if you care about the specific thing that you're producing as opposed to having something that passes whatever check, then that's maybe still some limitation.

44
00:04:22,029 --> 00:04:24,410
Though of course on the other hand it can still help you as well.

45
00:04:24,942 --> 00:04:26,770
I get a lot of feedback from it, that's good.

46
00:04:27,054 --> 00:04:35,970
I guess, do you guys feel like your students are working faster or producing better results now that we have these cool AI tools?

47
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:43,190
So I think, unlike maybe Vince, I think I don't ever write code anymore.

48
00:04:43,790 --> 00:04:47,490
I don't think any of my students write code, I'm fairly certain.

49
00:04:48,110 --> 00:04:54,150
I certainly see a whole lot of Codex and Claude sort of slop in their weekly updates.

50
00:04:55,770 --> 00:05:08,130
I think I write almost not at all with AI unless it's things that don't matter, like grant applications or not layers, I don't write with AI either.

51
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:16,430
Things that matter I don't write with AI, things that don't matter I of course write with AI because they don't matter, and so you're gonna just, you know, you're gonna do it.

52
00:05:17,678 --> 00:05:22,650
Code is really interesting because I really care about the results that come out of code but I don't care at all about what the code itself looks like.

53
00:05:22,650 --> 00:05:24,790
With writing, I really care what it actually looks like.

54
00:05:24,910 --> 00:05:28,390
That's sort of the thing that matters to me is the actual thought process.

55
00:05:28,878 --> 00:05:32,270
Plus, it's the fact that writing actually, by writing you think, right?

56
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:36,550
So, writing is the part, writing is the process of codifying thought in your own mind.

57
00:05:36,610 --> 00:05:41,050
And if you do value that, which maybe you do, maybe you don't, writing is a very good thing to do.

58
00:05:41,490 --> 00:05:46,450
So, oddly enough, for these writing models, the only thing I don't use them for is writing.

59
00:05:46,970 --> 00:05:48,570
I use them for basically everything else.

60
00:05:48,590 --> 00:05:55,390
So, you know, or, I mean, I have the dot and it, you know, does a lot of my sort of random management of email.

61
00:05:57,170 --> 00:05:59,830
I, yeah, everything else I'll use AI for except writing.

62
00:06:02,862 --> 00:06:12,170
To the point of teaching, and by the way, you, if you go on to do a degree, you know, to a software engineering job, you will also not write code.

63
00:06:12,250 --> 00:06:15,110
Maybe there are a few people that are still writing code right now that will go away very quickly.

64
00:06:15,610 --> 00:06:17,250
No one will write code in any software engineering job.

65
00:06:17,326 --> 00:06:17,790
Of course not.

66
00:06:18,650 --> 00:06:20,470
Despite that, you should all learn how to code.

67
00:06:21,070 --> 00:06:22,070
Genuinely, I think that.

68
00:06:23,070 --> 00:06:27,850
Learning how to code is, again, just like writing is about learning how to think, learning how to code is actually about learning how to think.

69
00:06:27,930 --> 00:06:31,250
You can think sequentially, you think about the...

70
00:06:33,670 --> 00:06:38,490
formulation of sequential logical thought into explicit instructions.

71
00:06:39,054 --> 00:06:44,490
That is a skill in and of itself, and I have always felt that I don't really understand something until I actually can code it myself.

72
00:06:44,990 --> 00:06:47,010
It is the only reason I code right now.

73
00:06:47,010 --> 00:06:53,310
I do not code for anything else besides trying to understand things myself, but you should all learn how to code for that exact same reason.

74
00:06:53,470 --> 00:06:56,356
You should learn how to understand things yourself, and that's a really useful way of doing it.

75
00:06:57,614 --> 00:06:59,530
But you will not code in an actual job.

76
00:07:00,110 --> 00:07:08,270
Much like you won't compute integrals in an actual job, but you should still learn how to compute integrals if you are interested in such things.

77
00:07:10,606 --> 00:07:11,770
I think I'll end there, actually.

78
00:07:11,810 --> 00:07:12,970
I don't want to drone on too much.

79
00:07:13,150 --> 00:07:14,692
There's lots more questions, so I'll turn it over.

80
00:07:14,830 --> 00:07:16,710
Daphne, do you have one comment you want to make?

81
00:07:17,490 --> 00:07:31,410
One of the reasons to learn to code, too, is AI is not perfect yet, although it's getting better, and I've had students use a lot of AI coding agents that help them write code, but at some point the AI coding agent is going to put in some subtle bug.

82
00:07:32,078 --> 00:07:42,950
And even if you're not writing code on a regular basis, being able to read the code that the AI produces and figure out what where the subtle bug is is I think a really important skill.

83
00:07:43,190 --> 00:07:49,188
And so this is another reason why you really should learn to at least understand the code that your AI agents are producing.

84
00:07:49,510 --> 00:07:52,070
And the best way to learn that is to learn to write the code yourself.

85
00:07:52,782 --> 00:07:56,650
I'll reiterate too what Zico said about writing.

86
00:07:57,430 --> 00:08:11,470
I've seen so many people now at all levels from the most senior to the most junior, like first year undergrads to all the way to like deans, writing stuff with AI that has no communicative value.

87
00:08:12,142 --> 00:08:17,970
And the goal of writing anything is to communicate, ideally to communicate to another human.

88
00:08:18,530 --> 00:08:25,410
And if you are sending something to another human that you expect them to read, it should have some value to them in reading it.

89
00:08:25,890 --> 00:08:34,210
And if you're not doing that, if you're just generating stuff with an AI that you barely proofread yourself, you are really doing a disservice to the world.

90
00:08:34,254 --> 00:08:37,289
You're wasting people's time and you're being disrespectful of their time.

91
00:08:37,730 --> 00:08:40,710
And so this is like really, really important to me to communicate.

92
00:08:41,010 --> 00:08:41,850
I actually want to jump in.

93
00:08:41,929 --> 00:08:42,110
Sorry.

94
00:08:42,270 --> 00:08:42,830
I'll let you go second.

95
00:08:43,049 --> 00:08:45,030
I love reading the results of AI.

96
00:08:45,650 --> 00:08:49,630
I do not care at all to read the results of your AI queries.

97
00:08:51,290 --> 00:08:54,310
I mean, I think that I'm happy to read the results.

98
00:08:54,430 --> 00:08:58,470
When I ask the question, do not ever want people to send me the results of their questions.

99
00:08:58,670 --> 00:09:00,580
I think that's called being the meat proxy.

100
00:09:00,830 --> 00:09:01,930
I've been

101
00:09:01,930 --> 00:09:02,930
the

102
00:09:02,930 --> 00:09:04,250
meat proxy too,

103
00:09:04,250 --> 00:09:06,950
Yeah, I think I'll share.

104
00:09:07,566 --> 00:09:08,690
I'm a little bit strange.

105
00:09:08,930 --> 00:09:14,790
My department has put up with me doing research in a very strange way, where I just try to figure out what the actual problems are in the world.

106
00:09:15,430 --> 00:09:17,530
For example, I spent fall break in Nigeria.

107
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:20,850
Then I came back from Nigeria, I taught some classes.

108
00:09:21,230 --> 00:09:22,628
I had a weekend, so I went to Boston.

109
00:09:22,790 --> 00:09:27,810
I gave a public lecture in Boston to a bunch of people, all different ages, from really young to 80 years old.

110
00:09:28,110 --> 00:09:29,370
Then I came back, I had to teach a class.

111
00:09:29,810 --> 00:09:31,530
The way we teach our class is we have dinner.

112
00:09:32,718 --> 00:09:33,190
I'm serious.

113
00:09:33,510 --> 00:09:34,150
Were you there?

114
00:09:34,330 --> 00:09:34,730
You were there.

115
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:35,050
Yeah, yeah.

116
00:09:35,270 --> 00:09:37,890
I see some people here in this room who are at that dinner.

117
00:09:38,170 --> 00:09:41,990
So we had dinner, and then after the dinner, I had to disappear because I had to go to Atlanta.

118
00:09:42,490 --> 00:09:49,890
On Monday, I gave a public lecture at UGA, Georgia, but I told them only on one condition, that on Tuesday, you set me up to go into some rural classroom.

119
00:09:50,286 --> 00:09:56,610
So I was teaching sixth graders yesterday morning, two different schools, and they introduced me to the kids as, hey, guys, you got a sub today.

120
00:09:56,670 --> 00:09:57,630
His name is Mr. Po.

121
00:09:58,910 --> 00:09:59,970
The kids have no idea.

122
00:10:00,090 --> 00:10:01,250
They still have no idea who I am.

123
00:10:01,262 --> 00:10:03,170
I don't want anyone telling them who I actually am.

124
00:10:03,230 --> 00:10:04,230
That will ruin the whole thing.

125
00:10:04,410 --> 00:10:11,630
But why I'm saying all of this is, actually, these days, if you can figure out what the real problems are, man, the AI is powerful.

126
00:10:12,090 --> 00:10:14,570
I actually have multiple AI agents running.

127
00:10:14,730 --> 00:10:15,990
Maybe not at this exact moment.

128
00:10:16,170 --> 00:10:18,730
But I have a computer, which has all these AI agents running.

129
00:10:18,810 --> 00:10:19,510
It's incredible now.

130
00:10:19,570 --> 00:10:23,910
If you have anything that you want to solve, thanks to these guys who are making this stuff, man, you can write code.

131
00:10:24,030 --> 00:10:25,110
You can create websites.

132
00:10:25,310 --> 00:10:27,770
You can create systems that will connect together lots of people.

133
00:10:28,090 --> 00:10:29,850
You can do video editing.

134
00:10:29,970 --> 00:10:31,030
I don't like video creation.

135
00:10:31,030 --> 00:10:33,390
and I think that looks a little fake, but video editing I'm happy to do.

136
00:10:33,810 --> 00:10:38,530
I've gotten to the point where, if I can just figure out what problem we're actually trying to solve, you're good.

137
00:10:38,870 --> 00:10:40,890
And in fact, in the math world, you already saw some of that.

138
00:10:40,970 --> 00:10:47,550
As soon as people figured out, man, you should just go and ask AI the Millennium Prize problems, because the word on the street is going to be, they're going to be solved soon.

139
00:10:47,690 --> 00:10:48,430
All right, let's go.

140
00:10:48,590 --> 00:10:50,830
You put 88 hours of compute, bang, you got it.

141
00:10:51,030 --> 00:10:55,430
But you see, the thing is, the most important bit of information was, Anthropic might have it soon.

142
00:10:55,570 --> 00:10:57,050
Even though they didn't have it.

143
00:10:57,070 --> 00:10:57,770
They didn't have it.

144
00:10:57,890 --> 00:11:00,410
But even with that little bit of information, you're like, now go.

145
00:11:00,410 --> 00:11:06,820
So, actually, the way I see it is that these days, the problems are the things that you need to be able to figure out.

146
00:11:07,374 --> 00:11:15,330
But the only way to figure out the problems is to understand the people, which is why I then spend my time doing this field research to understand what the heck are people even worrying about.

147
00:11:15,750 --> 00:11:20,290
In terms of teaching, my goal is to make it so that anyone in my class knows how to think.

148
00:11:20,530 --> 00:11:23,910
If you've taken my class this year, I'll tell you I taught it the same way 15 years ago.

149
00:11:25,390 --> 00:11:26,550
How long have I been here?

150
00:11:26,610 --> 00:11:27,430
Have I been here 15 years?

151
00:11:27,490 --> 00:11:27,990
I already forgot.

152
00:11:28,110 --> 00:11:29,330
But some number of years ago.

153
00:11:29,530 --> 00:11:31,590
Oh wait What year is it 2026?

154
00:11:31,694 --> 00:11:32,650
I've been here 16 years.

155
00:11:32,790 --> 00:11:33,290
What's wrong?

156
00:11:33,510 --> 00:11:41,230
Anyway, whatever But it's just that somehow the whole point of the class is hey Here's something you guys go think and brainstorm amongst yourselves that has not changed and in some sense.

157
00:11:41,270 --> 00:11:42,590
I think that that piece is timeless.

158
00:11:44,270 --> 00:11:49,630
Okay, so I'm going to slightly fast track and like, let's try and look at the future.

159
00:11:49,630 --> 00:11:53,090
So, Vince, you mentioned that, you know, there's some things that AI is helping with.

160
00:11:53,102 --> 00:11:57,490
Daphne, you mentioned like, some sometimes AI introduces bugs into code.

161
00:11:59,726 --> 00:12:06,590
Now, we're seeing AI solve longstanding open problems in mathematics, quantum algorithms.

162
00:12:08,078 --> 00:12:12,610
I guess, in what ways do you expect these gaps to close, if ever?

163
00:12:13,990 --> 00:12:17,670
And in what ways are you or your students still better than AI?

164
00:12:21,614 --> 00:12:26,090
Can we kind of like, what is the thing that we're still here for?

165
00:12:27,278 --> 00:12:28,990
It's a problem if we don't answer, right?

166
00:12:30,850 --> 00:12:35,430
No, but I think for now, lots of things are not that great for now.

167
00:12:35,730 --> 00:12:39,590
Writing of these models is still particularly bad in many ways, right?

168
00:12:39,590 --> 00:12:53,230
If you ask it to write a whole paper, and we all end up like reviewing a lot of these papers these days, they're really poorly written because they tend not to have a very good model of the reader and throw in all kinds of terminology that it knows, but we don't know.

169
00:12:53,646 --> 00:12:56,510
So, writing for human beings is definitely still a skill.

170
00:12:59,770 --> 00:13:02,030
Problem identification, I agree, is still important.

171
00:13:02,250 --> 00:13:05,410
I would also say it's not like 100% that it's all human.

172
00:13:06,070 --> 00:13:15,010
I had one example where I basically asked it, like, go find an interesting result in such and such area with a little bit of advice as to how to do it, and it did.

173
00:13:15,086 --> 00:13:19,270
But I didn't tell it what results to find.

174
00:13:19,430 --> 00:13:20,750
So that was interesting, I thought.

175
00:13:24,130 --> 00:13:33,010
Also, I think even the proofs that it finds, for now they often, they're really powering through and they're really painful proofs to read.

176
00:13:33,150 --> 00:13:36,190
Often there is a shorter proof if you really understand what's going on.

177
00:13:37,010 --> 00:13:46,630
And I think that the best proofs now are kind of like human-AI collaboration that they started, maybe AI managed to solve it but it was like not intelligible.

178
00:13:47,170 --> 00:13:49,060
And then people went back and forth with it.

179
00:13:49,430 --> 00:13:52,690
How it's gonna close, that's I think the really interesting question.

180
00:13:53,330 --> 00:13:56,250
And to some extent, I also am not completely sure.

181
00:13:56,870 --> 00:14:01,170
I think probably, I'm sure it will get more capable and more powerful.

182
00:14:01,838 --> 00:14:11,570
I don't have the best idea of whether it is relatively going to be more powering through and using kind of the same concepts that human beings already developed.

183
00:14:11,970 --> 00:14:24,890
I think for most of the resolved problems, actually, it's —Paul can contradict me, but my intuition is often when they resolved one of these open problems, it was often the direction that people were not pursuing.

184
00:14:25,390 --> 00:14:29,750
They were hoping there was a nice result, and then there's a contrary example, kind of powered through.

185
00:14:30,410 --> 00:14:36,710
And once you got through to AI writing, it wasn't like they had drastically new techniques, right?

186
00:14:36,730 --> 00:14:41,830
There was actually kind of human-type techniques that they managed to stitch together.

187
00:14:41,830 --> 00:14:44,290
That, I think, is, to me, the big open question.

188
00:14:44,390 --> 00:14:49,070
At what point is it going to develop like entirely new concepts from us?

189
00:14:49,090 --> 00:14:51,350
And then I think we're really like left in the dust.

190
00:14:52,270 --> 00:14:57,270
Or is it just sort of more power on the same kind of things that we already do?

191
00:14:57,430 --> 00:14:59,350
To me, that's one of the biggest open questions.

192
00:14:59,830 --> 00:15:00,750
And I don't know the answer.

193
00:15:03,780 --> 00:15:04,200
I know.

194
00:15:04,974 --> 00:15:05,320
Okay.

195
00:15:06,320 --> 00:15:12,620
Yeah, so I guess for me, doing research was always a combination of curiosity and problem solving.

196
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:16,180
And I think over time, like, the curiosity part still remains.

197
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:18,940
There are a lot of open questions, a lot of things to think about.

198
00:15:19,246 --> 00:15:23,740
And it's not just, like, oh, I was hoping to solve a problem, AI can solve it, and that's the end of my job.

199
00:15:23,820 --> 00:15:27,320
Like, I think there are always this, like, things that I was curious about.

200
00:15:27,440 --> 00:15:29,680
And to that degree, it still remains now.

201
00:15:29,780 --> 00:15:34,520
And in my group, especially, a lot of the work we do is figuring out what's the right way to formulate things.

202
00:15:34,580 --> 00:15:35,680
Like, what should we think about?

203
00:15:35,800 --> 00:15:39,740
And that has been accelerated with using AI to code and so on.

204
00:15:39,740 --> 00:15:46,620
but you can't offload that because I don't have a way to formulate the question that AI can just solve, and that's part of the process.

205
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:48,680
And I think that still remains.

206
00:15:49,326 --> 00:15:56,860
