00:49:56,690 --> 00:50:00,210
It's going to pay off much more later, I think, in your careers.

592
00:50:02,414 --> 00:50:04,190
Maybe I'll share a few things on this.

593
00:50:05,486 --> 00:50:14,390
I think, first of all, it's unhealthy to say, I like doing this thing, and I want to find a way to be able to keep doing this thing somehow.

594
00:50:14,610 --> 00:50:17,290
I'm actually not that fixed on what I like doing.

595
00:50:17,390 --> 00:50:19,650
In fact, I don't even mind if what I'm solving is a solved problem.

596
00:50:20,238 --> 00:50:22,470
I just like doing things that make other people's lives brighter.

597
00:50:23,566 --> 00:50:25,210
And you always have things that you can do there.

598
00:50:25,530 --> 00:50:35,430
Actually, what I'm saying here is that if you ever feel like I don't know what direction to go in, there's an easy thing to do, which is to try to figure out what would actually make a real difference in other people's lives.

599
00:50:35,950 --> 00:50:38,570
Interestingly enough, this lets you get ahead of broken systems.

600
00:50:39,342 --> 00:50:44,870
This is how you leapfrog, because the point is the system which may be broken is not delivering value.

601
00:50:45,198 --> 00:50:49,210
So you just figure out how to actually deliver that value, and then you'll be much, much better off.

602
00:50:49,454 --> 00:50:52,130
This is actually why you try to make sure that you have enough relationships.

603
00:50:52,290 --> 00:50:58,070
You can find out from other people what else is going on, the kinds of opportunities that you might get.

604
00:50:58,350 --> 00:50:59,770
They might not be the traditional ones.

605
00:51:00,030 --> 00:51:06,870
You might not be able to run through the traditional pathway of PhD, postdoc, academia, but maybe that's not even the right thing.

606
00:51:06,990 --> 00:51:07,430
Who knows?

607
00:51:07,502 --> 00:51:12,964
I mean, when I was going through my whole career, I actually never was saying I need to be in this place or in that place.

608
00:51:13,330 --> 00:51:19,290
It was always, let's see what we can do that's actually going to go and make some difference in people.

609
00:51:19,570 --> 00:51:22,690
Actually, if you know my background, I'm actually quite non-traditional.

610
00:51:23,050 --> 00:51:30,630
I actually went and I did research for a while, and then about 10 years ago, I decided to go and do crazy stuff, right?

611
00:51:30,910 --> 00:51:36,070
And now the thing is that when I was doing that, at the beginning, it was not at all clear that the reward function is going to work out that way.

612
00:51:36,110 --> 00:51:39,950
However, I was just thinking, this is actually what's going to make a difference in people's lives.

613
00:51:40,390 --> 00:51:44,270
And then at the end of the day, you end up okay, but I was saved actually by my coach number.

614
00:51:45,518 --> 00:51:46,130
This is true.

615
00:51:46,330 --> 00:51:54,750
It's because if you have enough people out there who know that you do good work, even when you're off the regular system, you actually do much better, right?

616
00:51:54,790 --> 00:51:55,690
You're actually still okay.

617
00:51:55,890 --> 00:51:59,410
It's just that a lot of people are more comfortable with trying to say, what are the rules?

618
00:51:59,570 --> 00:52:00,530
I'm going to follow these rules.

619
00:52:00,610 --> 00:52:01,230
I'm going to win.

620
00:52:01,430 --> 00:52:04,310
And somehow it's almost competitive of only a few people get through.

621
00:52:04,310 --> 00:52:06,290
Actually, that's not how I operate.

622
00:52:06,390 --> 00:52:08,490
How I operate is that there's all these problems to solve in the world.

623
00:52:08,590 --> 00:52:09,750
I don't need to be the best at it.

624
00:52:09,770 --> 00:52:11,590
I just need to solve a problem other people didn't solve.

625
00:52:11,650 --> 00:52:13,730
And there's like so many real problems in the world.

626
00:52:13,966 --> 00:52:15,428
Just go and grab one and solve it.

627
00:52:15,790 --> 00:52:25,990
And what gives you the freedom to do this is if you have this network of other folks who would support you, who think that, yeah, this person actually does good stuff even though he's doing things that are very, very strange.

628
00:52:26,510 --> 00:52:34,610
Something I'll share about the philosophy that I'm actually trying to propagate around the world is that the easier way to go through life is not to always be thinking saying, how do I get stuff?

629
00:52:35,110 --> 00:52:36,270
Like sometimes that's the mindset.

630
00:52:36,410 --> 00:52:37,630
How do I get a good life for myself?

631
00:52:37,710 --> 00:52:38,530
That's not how I operate.

632
00:52:38,890 --> 00:52:42,250
Actually, there's a kind of, there's a whole group of people I'm trying to promote.

633
00:52:42,478 --> 00:52:43,970
I'm trying to make more people like this.

634
00:52:44,070 --> 00:52:45,290
I call them thoughtful people.

635
00:52:45,450 --> 00:52:46,468
That's with two L's.

636
00:52:46,734 --> 00:52:49,230
If you're curious, go to thoughtful.com, there's a manifesto there.

637
00:52:49,610 --> 00:52:52,790
But so, there's two L's, okay, two L's, thoughtful.com with one L, something else.

638
00:52:53,030 --> 00:53:02,330
But if you go there, the concept is, there are people out there in the world whose reward function is actually to make other people's lives brighter, and also to understand things through their own geeking out and thinking.

639
00:53:02,330 --> 00:53:06,970
What I've been saying is the more people in the world who are like this, the more likely we'll be in good shape.

640
00:53:07,290 --> 00:53:12,690
And if you lead your life that way, you'll always be able to find some way to get through and find the other people who are like that.

641
00:53:12,750 --> 00:53:16,210
You'll work together and that's a way of navigating uncertainty.

642
00:53:22,734 --> 00:53:28,150
I'd like to maybe go down the sort of educational ladder all the way to undergrad.

643
00:53:28,410 --> 00:53:29,828
Many of us here are undergrads.

644
00:53:35,054 --> 00:53:48,380
So, you guys have talked about finding trust in other people and focusing on bettering people's lives, but the undergraduate education here at CMU is pretty much the same as it was five, 10 years ago.

645
00:53:49,262 --> 00:53:56,388
I'm wondering if the education here should change in response to these changing times.

646
00:54:00,520 --> 00:54:01,124
Yes.

647
00:54:03,080 --> 00:54:04,640
I can make a proposal.

648
00:54:05,040 --> 00:54:06,980
I already shared this proposal with some folks.

649
00:54:07,240 --> 00:54:22,000
What you should do is you should teach sort of normal stuff for the first two years, and then for the last two years, you should send seven passenger vans of students and faculty out to rural West Virginia, rural Pennsylvania, inner city Cleveland, and even our like Homewood and whatnot.

650
00:54:22,360 --> 00:54:28,780
These seven passenger vans should have five students who are from five different majors and two professors who are from two different departments.

651
00:54:28,910 --> 00:54:32,996
They go there, you land there, and you figure out what's wrong and you solve it.

652
00:54:33,742 --> 00:54:35,940
Now, I said all this and everyone thought I was totally nuts.

653
00:54:35,960 --> 00:54:45,820
And then, like, two weeks after I said that, it turns out that we are starting CMU, Miami, which is apparently having, which is apparently, which is true, which is a, yeah.

654
00:54:48,810 --> 00:54:51,110
But you know the model of CMU Miami, right?

655
00:54:51,310 --> 00:54:52,330
Like no majors.

656
00:54:52,470 --> 00:54:52,930
No departments.

657
00:54:53,030 --> 00:54:54,404
I was like, I wasn't that nuts

658
00:54:58,190 --> 00:55:00,680
Are we as extreme as Poe, yeah?

659
00:55:01,180 --> 00:55:07,660
The thing to understand, which is maybe going to be dissatisfying to all of you, is universities are by design slow.

660
00:55:08,320 --> 00:55:11,840
We're just slow at every type of change that you can imagine.

661
00:55:12,060 --> 00:55:16,040
And so there have been lots of discussions on how to change education.

662
00:55:16,220 --> 00:55:24,680
There's been lots of initiatives and efforts, and it is slowly percolating through the system in a way that is probably not going to meaningfully affect anybody in this room.

663
00:55:24,680 --> 00:55:36,020
but then if you have little siblings or eventually when you have children and they go into education at CMU, it's gonna look very different than the education that you are doing today.

664
00:55:36,398 --> 00:55:43,840
Just as I know you guys don't see it, but the education you guys are having today does look different than the education that people 50 years ago had at CMU.

665
00:55:44,600 --> 00:55:56,452
And so I think there's been a lot of people who are invested in changing things and it's just going to happen at a pace that is like not very visible to those of you who are only here for four years.

666
00:55:58,638 --> 00:56:04,580
But some of this I think can be student driven as well, right, in order to make us actually move and do new things.

667
00:56:06,446 --> 00:56:20,860
I think sort of in line with what Paul was saying, there's one kind of mindset of like, you have a particular track in mind and you wanna follow it and if something threatens it, you try to escape it, or maybe you don't go into computer science because it doesn't look like that's gonna give you the job.

668
00:56:21,518 --> 00:56:24,660
But there's also maybe kind of more running toward the problem, right?

669
00:56:24,700 --> 00:56:28,780
And so you might actually say, like, I would think computer science still is super interesting to study.

670
00:56:28,860 --> 00:56:29,800
Or mathematics, right?

671
00:56:29,800 --> 00:56:30,880
Because there's so much changing.

672
00:56:31,520 --> 00:56:36,040
And maybe there are problems, and maybe you can actually go and do something about the problem, right?

673
00:56:36,220 --> 00:56:43,340
You can join CASI, or whatever exactly your angle would be on what is going wrong and how you might fix it.

674
00:56:43,340 --> 00:56:54,160
And I think the kinds of people that actually go after the problems, sort of independently, maybe not exactly how anybody taught them to think, but have a new angle on it.

675
00:56:54,460 --> 00:57:00,240
And maybe you can bring some of us older faculty and other people on board with what you're doing.

676
00:57:02,062 --> 00:57:08,800
That's probably a much better path to a career than just trying to follow the path that had been laid out and is maybe going to get obsolete.

677
00:57:08,800 --> 00:57:09,560
All

678
00:57:09,560 --> 00:57:22,852
right, so to wrap things up, if you were a student today, I know we've talked a lot about advice, but just to summarize, if you were a student today, what is one piece of advice you would give to yourself?

679
00:57:25,486 --> 00:57:32,484
Maybe in like three sentences, we can think for a minute maybe, like an action item maybe.

680
00:57:40,340 --> 00:57:41,140
Computer science.

681
00:57:41,326 --> 00:57:52,240
I would have studied something that, like, I don't know, if you think about, like, like, what are things that we're absolutely going to need human expertise on in the future?

682
00:57:52,400 --> 00:57:53,720
Like civil engineering.

683
00:57:53,720 --> 00:58:02,380
We're going to need people who know how to build bridges, and I don't trust an AI to keep my bridge from falling, even if it's, like, the fanciest AI opening I can possibly build.

684
00:58:02,460 --> 00:58:04,940
I want a human to make sure my bridge is not going to fall.

685
00:58:06,286 --> 00:58:21,760
The other thing I would study, which is maybe, like, I already did kind of study these things, is find things that are that are just interesting, whereas I've been like increasingly collaborating with people who work in history, so like historians don't have a ground truth.

686
00:58:21,880 --> 00:58:26,720
They can't write code that, like write a proof that it can be automatically proved.

687
00:58:27,150 --> 00:58:34,960
Like how historians do research is they make an argument to you and also to other historians that I think the past went this way.

688
00:58:35,534 --> 00:58:41,080
And that's something that an AI can form an argument, but fundamentally that argument and is meant for human consumption.

689
00:58:41,680 --> 00:58:47,260
And so they are producing something that fundamentally has a human audience, and there is always going to be a human audience for.

690
00:58:47,840 --> 00:58:51,760
And that's the same as artists and musicians and things like that.

691
00:58:53,560 --> 00:59:03,240
I definitely in 10 years will trust an AI-generated bridge way more than I will trust a bridge generated by really fallible, tired humans that make mistakes all the time.

692
00:59:04,140 --> 00:59:08,196
Pittsburgh's a bad city to talk about bridge safety in also.

693
00:59:10,670 --> 00:59:15,220
I would definitely major in computer science again, to be clear.

694
00:59:17,020 --> 00:59:18,340
I was a double major.

695
00:59:18,400 --> 00:59:21,980
I majored in philosophy and computer science, which I probably would do again also.

696
00:59:23,740 --> 00:59:28,840
I find the codification of...

697
00:59:28,846 --> 00:59:31,380
Okay, so essentially, sorry, three sentences I know.

698
00:59:32,100 --> 00:59:32,340
It's okay.

699
00:59:34,222 --> 00:59:36,500
I really liked philosophy as an undergrad.

700
00:59:36,700 --> 00:59:37,400
I thought it was really cool.

701
00:59:37,400 --> 00:59:42,120
that was thinking about the problems of the mind, the most central problems we've ever thought about.

702
00:59:42,280 --> 00:59:56,740
And then I also sort of felt like, hey, well, we haven't really made all that much progress in the 2000 years or so we've been studying philosophy, whereas computer science, we're the ones that are actually gonna discover just the facts of the matter about thoughts very, very soon.

703
00:59:57,100 --> 01:00:01,660
I didn't think it would be this soon to be clear, but I felt like we were making a lot better progress than we are.

704
01:00:02,180 --> 01:00:05,280
I find something, I find it incredibly...

705
01:00:05,294 --> 01:00:11,360
So everyone here, and this is the course I teach and things like this, so I'm obviously biased here.

706
01:00:11,694 --> 01:00:14,480
Everyone here should write an LLM entirely from scratch.

707
01:00:14,830 --> 01:00:28,860
It's one of the most rewarding things you can possibly do because it basically says, here's a technology that is steamrolling down the nature of intelligent thought and you can write one in about 1,000 lines of Python code.

708
01:00:29,380 --> 01:00:30,560
That is wild.

709
01:00:30,960 --> 01:00:32,040
It is incredible.

710
01:00:32,040 --> 01:00:35,180
It is the most fun thing to realize.

711
01:00:35,340 --> 01:00:48,520
I mean, put aside all the other sort of consequences of this, which are obviously huge, it is fundamentally fun and mind-blowing to realize that you can actually write down the logic that captures intelligent thought.

712
01:00:48,880 --> 01:00:50,440
And it's not even that hard to do.

713
01:00:51,180 --> 01:00:52,260
That is wild.

714
01:00:52,880 --> 01:00:53,940
And that is why...

715
01:00:53,940 --> 01:00:58,280
And you can't do it without a computer science degree, by the way, because you won't actually understand those 100,000 lines of code.

716
01:00:58,380 --> 01:00:59,740
They're pretty complex lines of code.

717
01:01:00,380 --> 01:01:02,788
That is why you should major in computer science.

718
01:01:03,180 --> 01:01:13,340
Or do whatever else you find really enjoyable in terms of the things you can do with the skills you develop as a computer scientist, or whatever other field you're studying.

719
01:01:13,560 --> 01:01:17,200
I just, I think that is one of the most exciting things possible right now.

720
01:01:17,840 --> 01:01:19,260
I feel so guilty now.

721
01:01:19,340 --> 01:01:22,860
A couple of days ago, I asked ChatGPT, code an LLM from scratch.

722
01:01:23,620 --> 01:01:24,860
Do it yourself too.

723
01:01:25,580 --> 01:01:25,800
Sorry.

724
01:01:26,080 --> 01:01:26,940
I hurt myself.

725
01:01:26,940 --> 01:01:31,640
I like Zico's answer because he started with, oh, I definitely major in computer science again.

726
01:01:31,700 --> 01:01:33,260
Oh, wait, I didn't major in computer science.

727
01:01:33,360 --> 01:01:34,220
I know, it's a double major.

728
01:01:34,560 --> 01:01:35,760
Double major, okay, well.

729
01:01:36,302 --> 01:01:50,000
But really, I think what that shows is, Zico is, he was just interested in stuff, not for the label of being a computer scientist, but he found it inherently interesting, like thinking about the nature of thought and so on.

730
01:01:50,480 --> 01:01:52,820
And I think that's a much better way to be.

731
01:01:53,240 --> 01:01:59,360
Maybe like CMU Miami is gonna try to be that way, organized around challenges, I think it's a good thing to try.

732
01:02:00,200 --> 01:02:07,180
But in either way, it's going to be somewhat of a crutch, like, oh, I'm a CS major, that's how I orient myself, oh, it's like this societal challenge.

733
01:02:07,700 --> 01:02:18,820
Hopefully you have the thing that you think is important, the problem that you want to run towards, and that fascinates you, and then hopefully some of the majors and stuff kind of fits well with that.

734
01:02:19,854 --> 01:02:27,172
But I would recommend finding the thing that you're passionate about, and then figuring out how to tie all the other stuff to that.

735
01:02:30,318 --> 01:02:33,660
Yeah, I guess I kind of agree with the general sentiment here.

736
01:02:34,040 --> 01:02:39,080
It's definitely a period of extreme uncertainty and change, and it can be terrifying.

737
01:02:39,900 --> 01:02:48,320
And I guess the positive side of that is some of the things that we would usually do, like we had some metrics that we felt we had to optimize and career build, and they're all breaking down.

738
01:02:48,480 --> 01:02:54,060
So it's, in a way, an excuse to really follow your passion and do what you think is fun and interesting.

739
01:02:56,654 --> 01:03:08,140
Just to be an optimist, I see some of this even in people around me and my students and so on, like people are less inclined to prioritize publishing something over helping their friends or mentoring students.

740
01:03:08,260 --> 01:03:13,400
So there was a period where some of my grad students felt like it's not worth having undergrads helping because Claude is better.

741
01:03:13,742 --> 01:03:23,380
But then over time, they're also like, okay, you know what, I actually just want to chat with people and brainstorm and have ideas, whether I get two NeurIPS papers or three is not making a big difference to my life.

742
01:03:23,380 --> 01:03:33,060
And so, I think we are kind of at that point where, since all of these external metrics are anyway breaking down, really double down on doing the things that you think are interesting.

743
01:03:33,400 --> 01:03:47,180
And I still feel like there's a space for curiosity-driven work, and it's not all about solving whether you're the first to solve a problem, it's also about whether you had fun solving it, what did you learn, and those are going to become more important as more things are automated.

744
01:03:49,102 --> 01:03:49,680
Which major?

745
01:03:49,780 --> 01:03:50,220
I don't know.

746
01:03:50,220 --> 01:03:53,060
But I liked computer science because it was fun, so I would do that again.

747
01:03:54,350 --> 01:04:01,160
So you might have noticed that whenever you give me a mic, my goal is to give you the most unusual, surprising answer that might actually be true, OK?

748
01:04:01,220 --> 01:04:05,380
Because that tries to increase the information content as much as possible.

749
01:04:05,480 --> 01:04:12,388
So I'll tell you, actually for me, the single most important thing that happened in college is on the first day of college, I met my wife.

750
01:04:13,560 --> 01:04:16,480
And then the day before we graduated, we got married.

751
01:04:18,060 --> 01:04:19,275
So

752
01:04:19,275 --> 01:04:21,170
that's pretty good value.

753
01:04:21,170 --> 01:04:21,790
I mean,

754
01:04:29,422 --> 01:04:32,140
so that's why we've been together for, I don't know, how many years, I got to subtract.

755
01:04:32,334 --> 01:04:32,760
It's too hard.

756
01:04:33,140 --> 01:04:33,860
Then you'll know I'm old.

757
01:04:33,940 --> 01:04:35,440
This is 20-something years, right?

758
01:04:35,500 --> 01:04:38,500
But the point is, then we end up working together on all kinds of different things.

759
01:04:38,560 --> 01:04:41,780
Some of you actually know her because we work together on all the outreach work that I do.

760
01:04:42,100 --> 01:04:46,960
But basically why I'm saying this is that somehow it's these human relationships, right?

761
01:04:47,374 --> 01:04:50,060
I actually have been thinking, what's the value of the college experience?

762
01:04:50,240 --> 01:04:53,000
Someday you'll get a better education from asking Chad GPT.

763
01:04:53,000 --> 01:04:58,820
But what you can't get is you can't come into a room like this and find out these are people I should go to dinner with.

764
01:04:59,598 --> 01:05:06,980
Because these are the people in this town, in this university, who thought it was interesting enough to listen to a bunch of weird people give you ideas.

765
01:05:07,340 --> 01:05:09,420
So you might actually want to talk to these people.

766
01:05:09,620 --> 01:05:13,800
You don't have to get married to them, but you might actually enjoy meeting them.

767
01:05:14,020 --> 01:05:17,860
To me, the value of a university with a big room like this, it's a giant mixer.

768
01:05:18,420 --> 01:05:19,748
So enjoy the opportunity you have.

769
01:05:21,614 --> 01:05:26,400
All right, and with that, I want to thank our faculty panelists, this amazing panel.

770
01:05:37,350 --> 01:05:42,630
I do want to give my own little piece of advice, if I can, I prepared a little statement.

771
01:05:43,598 --> 01:05:50,030
So I'd like to end on a bit of reflection, I've been at CMU for a while, I just started my fifth year master's here, actually.

772
01:05:50,930 --> 01:05:55,850
And I've seen certainly myself and others pour their heart into the work at CMU.

773
01:05:56,494 --> 01:06:08,836
But with every passing semester, I've seen people's hearts simply become the work, to the point where their hearts no longer resemble what was when they were entering CMU, when they were entering high school, when they were kids.

774
01:06:09,670 --> 01:06:20,890
And I wish for everyone to remember that intense curiosity they had when they were kids, their aspirations to discover and uncover great mysteries, and see it through.

775
01:06:21,210 --> 01:06:24,036
Don't lose that while you're in college when there's so much going on.

776
01:06:24,590 --> 01:06:38,820
And I was inspired by the wonderful speakers today, and I hope you take the prospect of a very variable and changing future seriously and think a little bit more about what you can do for each other and for the future, and don't lose your heart in the work.

777
01:06:39,150 --> 01:06:40,430
So yeah, thank you.

778
01:06:45,500 --> 01:06:51,012
There will be doughnuts, and if any of the faculty would like to stay around, you can chat with them.

779
01:06:51,180 --> 01:06:53,580
But yes, we have a lot of doughnuts.

780
01:06:53,640 --> 01:06:54,360
So please take them

